// Cutting many small pieces out of one large string.
//
// This is what a lexer does: the source is one String, and every token is a slice of it.
// A `slice` implemented as "read the whole object, then take the range" costs the *source's*
// length per call rather than the result's, so a file of n bytes yielding n/k tokens costs
// n^2/k -- and the cost is invisible in any test whose strings are short.
//
// The virtual machine had exactly that, and it made the VM eight times slower than the
// tree-walking interpreter it exists to beat: 48 seconds where native code took 0.4. The
// interpreter never had it (its String is a Go string, and a Go slice is free) and neither
// does native code (`rt_str_slice_into` copies the result's own bytes and no more).
//
// This case is here for the *answer* -- three engines, three levels, the same 65,536 bytes
// sliced out -- and for the cost pressure: 8,192 slices of eight bytes is 64 KiB of copying
// when the cost follows the result, and a gigabyte of byte-at-a-time reads when it follows
// the source, which is thirty-five times slower on the virtual machine. The complexity
// itself is asserted where it can actually fail a test rather than merely slow one down:
// `internal/vm`'s TestSlicingCostDoesNotFollowTheSourcesLength compares the per-slice cost
// out of a 1 KiB string against the same slices out of a 128 KiB one.
use std::io;

fn main() {
    // 128 KiB, built by doubling so that the construction itself is linear-ish.
    let mut s = "0123456789abcdef";
    let mut k = 0;
    while k < 13 {
        s = s.concat(s);
        k = k + 1;
    }
    io::println("\(s.len())");

    // Eight bytes at a time, from every part of it.
    let mut total = 0;
    let mut i = 0;
    while i < 8192 {
        let at = (i * 16) % (s.len() - 8);
        total = total + s.slice(at, at + 8).len();
        i = i + 1;
    }
    io::println("\(total)");
}
